1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,367
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. Every AI system that produces an answer

2
00:00:03,367 --> 00:00:06,700
eventually needs a score attached to it — a confidence percentage, a

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:07,767
pass or fail, a grade.

4
00:00:08,533 --> 00:00:11,933
Here's the question almost nobody asks before trusting that number: who, or

5
00:00:11,933 --> 00:00:14,333
what, actually produced it — and can that thing be trusted more

6
00:00:14,333 --> 00:00:17,333
than the system it's grading? Today: a teardown of grading the machine

7
00:00:17,333 --> 00:00:18,133
itself.

8
00:00:18,167 --> 00:00:21,167
The obvious answer — ask the AI how confident it is,

9
00:00:21,733 --> 00:00:24,100
or ask a second AI to check the first one's work —

10
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:26,000
turns out to be broken in a specific,

11
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:30,400
well-documented way. And the fix is less glamorous than it sounds: mostly

12
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,467
boring, structural, computed arithmetic.

13
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:34,967
Which turns out to be exactly why it works.

14
00:00:35,730 --> 00:00:37,697
The simplest approach is also the most tempting:

15
00:00:38,063 --> 00:00:40,530
just have the model tell you how sure it is. Append a

16
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:42,463
confidence score to the output — 'I'm 92%

17
00:00:42,863 --> 00:00:45,597
confident in this conclusion' — and use that number downstream.

18
00:00:46,163 --> 00:00:49,297
Here's the problem, and it's not minor: the same mechanism that produced

19
00:00:49,297 --> 00:00:52,997
a wrong answer is the mechanism producing the confidence score attached to

20
00:00:52,997 --> 00:00:53,797
it.

21
00:00:53,497 --> 00:00:56,597
A model that confidently hallucinated a number has no separate,

22
00:00:57,097 --> 00:01:00,997
more-honest module standing off to the side, watching itself, immune to the

23
00:01:00,997 --> 00:01:02,097
exact same failure.

24
00:01:02,463 --> 00:01:03,363
It's the same fluent,

25
00:01:03,763 --> 00:01:05,297
pattern-completing process either way.

26
00:01:05,797 --> 00:01:09,663
Self-reported confidence measures how confident the text sounds. It does not measure

27
00:01:09,663 --> 00:01:13,697
how well-founded the underlying claim actually is. Those two things correlate

28
00:01:13,697 --> 00:01:14,730
loosely at best.

29
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:16,150
So:

30
00:01:15,883 --> 00:01:19,583
self-graded confidence is biased, because the same process makes the mistake and

31
00:01:19,583 --> 00:01:20,383
grades it.

32
00:01:20,717 --> 00:01:24,017
A second AI judge is a category error — a good rationalization

33
00:01:24,017 --> 00:01:26,383
and genuine reasoning read identically from outside.

34
00:01:26,783 --> 00:01:30,783
A computed score, built from fixed deductions on observable facts, is inspectable

35
00:01:30,883 --> 00:01:32,617
— and it's the only one of the three where you can

36
00:01:32,617 --> 00:01:36,017
actually go find a mistake and fix it. It's still being calibrated.

37
00:01:36,283 --> 00:01:37,383
That's the honest state of it.

38
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:41,107
Your turn. Find one place your own system produces a confidence score

39
00:01:41,107 --> 00:01:41,907
or a grade.

40
00:01:42,307 --> 00:01:45,373
Ask: is it self-reported by the same model that produced the answer?

41
00:01:45,773 --> 00:01:48,207
Is it graded by a second model judging plausibility?

42
00:01:48,573 --> 00:01:50,873
Or is it computed from fixed, observable deductions?

43
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:54,273
If it's either of the first two, sketch what an inspectable, computed

44
00:01:54,273 --> 00:01:57,107
version would actually look like for your case — even three or

45
00:01:57,107 --> 00:01:58,673
four penalty rules is a start.

46
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:02,257
That's the short version. The full video also covers So the next

47
00:02:02,257 --> 00:02:05,357
instinct is:…, So if you can't trust… and Here's the part that's

48
00:02:05,357 --> 00:02:08,157
easy… — watch Grading the Machine for the whole story.

49
00:02:08,623 --> 00:02:09,723
The link is right below.
